Patch 06b: orientation partition pruning, frame-aware and noise-gated.

Supersedes patch 06 (0006-ext-partition-texture-pruning.patch), which CTC
round 1 measured at +14.00% / +0.75% BD-YUV on A1 (ratio 18.7) and
+18.52% / +1.29% on A2 (ratio 14.4), against a Speed-4 bar of 20.

The binding constraint is A2. At unchanged speed it needs BD 1.29% -> 0.926%
(-28%); A1 needs only 0.75% -> 0.700% (-6.7%).

A2 scored worse than A1 while pruning MORE. Uniform over-pruning would have
cost both classes alike, so the extra loss on the longer 33-frame GOP is more
consistent with a bad partition choice persisting once later frames predict
from it. Two changes follow, neither of which is a blunt reduction in pruning
strength:

1. Absolute variance floor (PART_PROFILE_MIN_VAR). The orientation test was a
   pure ratio, so on flat blocks it compared noise against noise and could
   prune on an 8:1 ratio between two single-digit variances. Flat blocks
   settle on PARTITION_NONE quickly regardless, so this should return
   BD-rate at very little speed cost.

2. Frame-aware evidence bar (orientation_anisotropy_for_frame). Pruning is
   disabled on key frames and the top ARF layer, doubled in strictness on the
   layer below, and unchanged on the leaves that nothing predicts from.

Both thresholds are first principled guesses and want a sweep, not a defence.
Expected direction: lower speedup than patch 06, disproportionately lower
BD-rate. Whether the ratio actually clears 20 is the question for the next
round -- this patch is a hypothesis, not a result.

